          title: "The mountain of the unveiling",
          body: "**Μετεμορφώθη** — he was *metamorphosed*, form itself changing — and Matthew's images run outward from the face: it shone **like the sun**, not reflecting light (Moses' face shone from exposure, Exodus 34) but sourcing it. The staging is built from Sinai parts: a high mountain, six days (Exodus 24:16), a bright cloud that *overshadows* (ἐπεσκίασεν — the glory-cloud word), a voice. And the two visitors are not random celebrities: **Moses and Elijah** are the Law and the Prophets in person — the only two men in the Old Testament who met God on a mountain, one denied entry to the land, the other taken without dying; between them, all of Israel's Scripture stands talking with Jesus. Peter, terrified into hospitality, proposes three tents — one each, a shrine row, Jesus filed among the greats as an equal. The cloud interrupts him *while he is still speaking*, and the voice repeats the baptismal formula with one addition aimed straight at the tent-plan: **ἀκούετε αὐτοῦ, listen to him** — not *them*; the two supporting witnesses have already vanished when the disciples look up, leaving *Jesus only*. Placed here, a week after the first passion prediction, the scene has a pastoral job: three men have just been told their Messiah will be killed, and before that road begins they are shown, once, what walks it. The glory is real and prior; the cross will not be an accident happening *to* this face. They are ordered to silence until the resurrection — because until then, the story of the shining face would only be misunderstood as the story of a Messiah who does not lose.",
