      annotations: [
        {
          id: 'a17.7.1',
          kind: "concept",
          refs: ['g17.7.6', 'g17.8.11'],
          lemma: "ἁψάμενος αὐτῶν … Ἰησοῦν μόνον",
          title: "The touch after the terror — and “Jesus only”",
          body: "Mark this small sequence, because it is the whole gospel in three verses. The theophany does what theophanies do: the disciples are flattened, faces to the rock, **terribly afraid** — the standard human response to unscreened glory, from Sinai onward. And then something no Old Testament theophany contains: footsteps, and **a touch**. Ἁψάμενος — the same verb that reached the leper and the fever — the voice from the cloud has walked over and put a hand on them, with the words this gospel keeps giving to the terrified: *rise, and do not fear.* The unbearable glory turns out to have hands. When they dare to look up, the summary is two words that have served contemplatives for centuries: **Ἰησοῦν μόνον** — *Jesus only*. Moses gone, Elijah gone, cloud gone, shining gone; what remains of the entire apocalypse is the ordinary-looking friend helping them off the ground. That is Matthew's theology of revelation in miniature: everything the Law, the Prophets, and the glory-cloud had to say has resolved into this one touchable person — and when visions fade, as they do, what faith is left holding is not the memory of light but him.",
        },
      ],
